  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry for tonight and Monday, but the potential \nfor T-storms with heavy rain and damaging winds returns on \nTuesday.\n\nSFC high pressure over Eastern Lower Michigan this evening will\ndrift SE over PA tonight and Monday bringing generally clear\nskies, light wind, and comfortable temps and dewpoints.\n\nStratus/stratocu deck (and perhaps some showers) advects north \nacross the eastern part of the CWA tomorrow night into Tuesday,\nwhile some increase in mid and high clouds occurs over the\nWestern half of PA.\n\nNo real change to the day 3 outlook for Tuesday for storms.\nMade a storm total grid for rainfall for Tuesday, heaviest\namts on average across the east, where dewpoints fcst to come\nup quickly into the 70s on Tuesday.\n\nMore information below.\n\nTuesday looks somewhat similar to what we had yesterday \n(Saturday) with high PW values, and strong dynamics, as an \nabnormally deep, sub-100mb low tracks just north of the Lower \nGreat Lakes with a deepening upper level trough and left exit\nregion/strong upper level diffluent flow moving over the CWA at\npeak heating into Tuesday evening. \n\nWe've already had 2 similarly deep systems this summer and  \nthis would be the third one. Main threat from this will likely \nbe strong storms with the potential for damaging winds. Also \nheavy rain, but like Saturday, speed of storms would likely \nlimit the risk of widespread hydro problems. \n\nMain concern with hydro is having the activity in the same \nlocations as where the rain was on Saturday. Some areas \nyesterday had 1 to 2 plus inches of rain, while some spots \nacross the north and south had next to nothing. Some areas too \nwet so far this July, while others are still too dry from the \nlack of rain in June.\n\nAfter mid week, temperatures will trend near to a bit below \nnormal, as we head into the end of July.",
